<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abdirahman Ahmed Ali Tuur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1931">1931</a>–<a href="/years/2003">2003</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abdirahman Ahmed Ali Tuur was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> politician and chairman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somali-national-movement">Somali National Movement</a> (SNM). After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siad-barre">Siad Barre</a> he presided over the declaration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a>'s independence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burao">Burao</a> in May 1991 and became its first president. He was replaced at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borama">Borama</a> conference of 1993 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-haji-ibrahim-egal">Muhammad Haji Ibrahim Egal</a> and later briefly supported reunion with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abdirahman-ahmed-ali-tuur.jpg" alt="Abdirahman Ahmed Ali Tuur" width="311" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mohamed Badri Mohamed · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abdirahman_Ahmed_Ali_Tuur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tuur was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burao">Burao</a>, in the British Somaliland Protectorate, on 6 November 1931, and came from the Muse Arreh sub-clan of the Habar Yoonis. His name, also written Tur or Tour, means hunchback. One of the best students of the protectorate's first intermediate school, he won a scholarship in 1948 to the Hantoob Secondary School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, where he befriended future Sudanese leaders such as Jaafar Nimeiry, Sadiq Al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> and Hassan Al-Turabi and excelled at football and athletics. A further scholarship took him to the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exeter">Exeter</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He began as an administrative officer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borama">Borama</a> in 1956, became district commissioner in 1959, governor of the Eastern Region at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burao">Burao</a> in 1961 and of the Western Region at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hargeisa">Hargeisa</a> in 1964. He then joined the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and served as ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> and the UAE between 1964 and 1983.</p><p>He went on to lead the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somali-national-movement">Somali National Movement</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> force drawn mainly from the Isaaq clan, which sought to overthrow the military regime of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siad-barre">Siad Barre</a>. Founded with a unionist constitution, the movement later turned to secession, and under his leadership the northwestern territories declared independence on 18 May 1991.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After his presidency he renounced the separatist platform in 1994 and called for reconciliation with the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> in a federal system with shared power, speaking for many Isaaq who opposed secession; he also gave some support to the UNOSOM mission in the south. The government of Mohamed Haji Ibrahim Egal sentenced him to death in absentia for treason. He joined the government of Mohamed Farrah Aidid in Mogadishu, then after disagreements lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> until 2001, when he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a> and declared that he accepted secession. In the 2003 presidential election he campaigned for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dahir-riyale-kahin">Dahir Riyale Kahin</a>. He died on 8 November 2003 and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burao">Burao</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abdirahman Tuur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The SNM chairman <b>Abdirahman Ahmed Ali Tuur</b> became the first president, and a second conference at Borama in 1993 transferred power to Muhammad Haji Ibrahim Egal, a former prime minister of the Somali Republic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-somaliland">Republic of Somaliland · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burao">Burao</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hargeisa">Hargeisa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=9.53,45.54&amp;year=1977" aria-label="Burao on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Abdirahman Ahmed Ali Tuur"><image href="/maps/globes/g06000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3793 4189h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3793 4189h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=9.53,45.54&amp;year=1977">What was here in 1977? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
